1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,000
They're a cut above.

2
00:00:03,001 --> 00:00:09,000
They can split a single hair or a head of lettuce,

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:13,000
lacerate entire trees in the blink of an eye,

4
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:18,000
carve through hard steel, or edge it with delicate precision,

5
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:23,000
chopping, slicing, cutting, or biting.

6
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:25,000
Watch your fingers.

7
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:29,000
Now, the world's sharpest on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
What is the world's sharpest?

9
00:00:45,640 --> 00:00:47,660
Is it the finest edge?

10
00:00:47,760 --> 00:00:50,560
Is it the ease by which one material can slice or puncture

11
00:00:50,660 --> 00:00:52,560
another?

12
00:00:52,660 --> 00:00:56,260
Or is it something that won't dull with repeated use?

13
00:00:56,360 --> 00:01:00,000
The answer, of course, depends on what needs to be cut.

14
00:01:00,001 --> 00:01:07,000
This gracefully curved sword is called the katana.

15
00:01:07,001 --> 00:01:13,440
It rose to distinction in 16th century feudal Japan,

16
00:01:13,441 --> 00:01:17,920
wielded by the legendary samurai warriors.

17
00:01:17,921 --> 00:01:21,880
They alone were permitted to carry the katana,

18
00:01:21,881 --> 00:01:25,960
and its mystique grew during an era of civil war

19
00:01:25,961 --> 00:01:30,000
as they used it to die the katana.

20
00:01:30,001 --> 00:01:33,100
and dismember their enemies.

21
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:35,840
It is renowned as the world's sharpest weapon for slicing

22
00:01:35,940 --> 00:01:37,400
through human flesh and bone.

23
00:01:45,040 --> 00:01:47,880
This tough as bone bamboo shoot proves too difficult to cut

24
00:01:47,980 --> 00:01:56,360
through in a single swipe for a hatchet or a machete.

25
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:03,500
But one swing by the katana blade unerringly severs the chute.

26
00:02:09,840 --> 00:02:11,240
Or even two at once.

27
00:02:18,060 --> 00:02:19,760
These five-inch thick woven mats are

28
00:02:19,860 --> 00:02:22,060
said to be as tough to cut through as a human leg.

29
00:02:25,460 --> 00:02:28,200
But this sword, designed to slice through a torso

30
00:02:28,201 --> 00:02:32,000
from collarbone to hip in a single swipe

31
00:02:32,100 --> 00:02:33,100
severs them easily.

32
00:02:38,580 --> 00:02:42,680
Sharpness is one thing, and strength and durability

33
00:02:42,780 --> 00:02:44,620
and flexibility is another.

34
00:02:44,720 --> 00:02:47,260
The Japanese sword just happens to have all of that all together

35
00:02:47,360 --> 00:02:48,480
in one.

36
00:02:48,580 --> 00:02:51,320
So can the Japanese sword shave the hair off your arm?

37
00:02:51,420 --> 00:02:52,600
Yes, it can.

38
00:02:52,700 --> 00:02:54,300
Is it as sharp as a razor blade?

39
00:02:54,400 --> 00:02:55,600
Yes, it is.

40
00:02:55,700 --> 00:02:57,400
But can a razor blade cut through a helmet?

41
00:02:57,401 --> 00:02:58,900
No.

42
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:00,900
The Japanese, being the perfectionists that they are,

43
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:04,440
spent an awful lot of time making it the perfect cutting

44
00:03:04,540 --> 00:03:05,400
tool.

45
00:03:05,500 --> 00:03:08,320
The curvature on the edge allows you to swing straight

46
00:03:08,420 --> 00:03:11,940
and still use a slicing motion.

47
00:03:12,040 --> 00:03:13,780
It's what we call differentially tempered,

48
00:03:13,880 --> 00:03:15,820
which means the edge is much harder than the back.

49
00:03:15,920 --> 00:03:18,320
So the back can act as a shock absorber,

50
00:03:18,420 --> 00:03:21,560
but the edge doesn't get soft when you cut it too often.

51
00:03:21,660 --> 00:03:23,400
It stays sharp.

52
00:03:23,500 --> 00:03:25,560
Indeed, the edge on a well-crafted katana

53
00:03:25,561 --> 00:03:26,800
never need sharpening.

54
00:03:30,700 --> 00:03:32,800
How did the katana's makers achieve this deadly

55
00:03:32,900 --> 00:03:36,000
and enduring sharpness?

56
00:03:36,100 --> 00:03:38,100
There's a clue in what appear to be blemishes

57
00:03:38,200 --> 00:03:39,500
on the katana blade's face.

58
00:03:42,320 --> 00:03:46,320
Swordsmith K.C. Lund will show the significance of these marks

59
00:03:46,420 --> 00:03:50,260
as he fashions a katana using the same process originated

60
00:03:50,360 --> 00:03:52,520
a millennium ago by Japanese artisans.

61
00:03:55,560 --> 00:03:59,160
His initial steps are much like those followed by European

62
00:03:59,260 --> 00:04:01,460
swordsmiths.

63
00:04:01,560 --> 00:04:04,700
Lund begins by hammering steel chips together

64
00:04:04,800 --> 00:04:08,500
and shaping them into a bar.

65
00:04:08,600 --> 00:04:12,320
Then he repeatedly folds the bar in half,

66
00:04:12,420 --> 00:04:15,780
hammering and elongating the steel until he's created

67
00:04:15,880 --> 00:04:17,220
the katana's basic shape.

68
00:04:20,760 --> 00:04:25,560
Now Lund performs a crucial step unique to the Japanese process.

69
00:04:25,561 --> 00:04:29,060
He begins to coat the back of the blade in clay.

70
00:04:29,061 --> 00:04:31,560
The clay will insulate the underlying steel

71
00:04:31,561 --> 00:04:35,560
from the full effects of a subsequent heat treatment.

72
00:04:35,561 --> 00:04:39,100
It will alter the microstructure of the steel

73
00:04:39,101 --> 00:04:41,600
and harden the blade.

74
00:04:41,601 --> 00:04:44,140
The exposed steel will harden completely in order

75
00:04:44,141 --> 00:04:47,720
to support a razor sharp edge.

76
00:04:47,721 --> 00:04:51,560
When a steel becomes harder, it becomes more brittle,

77
00:04:51,561 --> 00:04:56,900
becomes harder, it becomes more brittle, more like glass.

78
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:58,560
Even the little pieces of clay

79
00:04:58,660 --> 00:05:00,900
will leave a somewhat softer area.

80
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:04,800
The hope is that this is going to prevent any type of cracks

81
00:05:04,900 --> 00:05:07,600
that might run down the length of the blade.

82
00:05:07,700 --> 00:05:10,620
Lund now performs the hardening process,

83
00:05:10,720 --> 00:05:12,680
first by heating the clay-coated blade

84
00:05:12,780 --> 00:05:17,220
to 1,500 degrees Fahrenheit, then plunging it

85
00:05:17,320 --> 00:05:20,620
into a cool salt bath.

86
00:05:20,621 --> 00:05:23,520
This produces the trademark scars along the blade.

87
00:05:26,520 --> 00:05:29,360
And since the leading edge expands more than the trailing

88
00:05:29,460 --> 00:05:34,060
edge during the treatment, the blade becomes curved.

89
00:05:34,160 --> 00:05:36,740
It all combines to create a durable, deep slicing

90
00:05:36,840 --> 00:05:39,180
instrument.

91
00:05:39,280 --> 00:05:41,280
But it's the final crucial touches

92
00:05:41,380 --> 00:05:45,780
that turn this edge into the world's sharpest sword.

93
00:05:45,880 --> 00:05:48,880
Now that the blade is hardened on the edge,

94
00:05:48,881 --> 00:05:51,280
softer on the back. We need to profile this edge

95
00:05:51,380 --> 00:05:54,580
and bring it down into a slight convex edge.

96
00:05:54,680 --> 00:05:57,280
It'll be done on a water stone, which is the way it's been done

97
00:05:57,380 --> 00:06:00,320
for hundreds of years.

98
00:06:00,420 --> 00:06:02,920
In addition to its characteristic hard edge,

99
00:06:03,020 --> 00:06:05,900
the convex shape of the katana blade also sets it apart

100
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:09,740
from European models.

101
00:06:09,840 --> 00:06:11,940
It really comes down to blade geometry,

102
00:06:12,040 --> 00:06:16,380
and that has a lot to do with the ability of a sword

103
00:06:16,381 --> 00:06:18,880
to cut through a target.

104
00:06:18,881 --> 00:06:24,380
Usually, your European blade, the body would be like this.

105
00:06:24,381 --> 00:06:26,380
You've got a very thin edge.

106
00:06:26,381 --> 00:06:28,380
It doesn't have much supporting it.

107
00:06:28,381 --> 00:06:31,380
And what that causes is quite a bit of resistance

108
00:06:31,381 --> 00:06:33,380
as it cuts through an object.

109
00:06:33,381 --> 00:06:37,380
Whereas a Japanese sword, they're fairly thick blades,

110
00:06:37,381 --> 00:06:39,380
and they were convex ground.

111
00:06:39,381 --> 00:06:41,380
So as they go through a target,

112
00:06:41,381 --> 00:06:44,380
the stuff they're cutting tends to peel off.

113
00:06:44,381 --> 00:06:48,320
The whole blade isn't dragging through the item it's cutting.

114
00:06:48,420 --> 00:06:53,180
Plus, they have a lot of support behind the edge of the blade

115
00:06:53,280 --> 00:06:56,200
to give it more strength.

116
00:06:56,300 --> 00:07:00,260
So is the katana truly the sharpest sword?

117
00:07:00,360 --> 00:07:03,140
The proof of that is that Japanese swords

118
00:07:03,240 --> 00:07:05,840
haven't changed in their construction

119
00:07:05,940 --> 00:07:08,240
for over 1,000 years.

120
00:07:08,340 --> 00:07:10,800
If there were any modern technological improvements

121
00:07:10,900 --> 00:07:14,380
to be made, steel or anything, it would have been done.

122
00:07:14,381 --> 00:07:18,220
Today, the most vicious enemy any of us attack with a razor

123
00:07:18,320 --> 00:07:20,420
sharp blade might be a common vegetable.

124
00:07:24,320 --> 00:07:26,920
But there's no need to carry a 20 plus inch katana blade

125
00:07:27,020 --> 00:07:28,020
into the kitchen.

126
00:07:30,220 --> 00:07:32,440
Cutco Cutlery of Oleann, New York,

127
00:07:32,540 --> 00:07:36,740
claims to create the sharpest kitchen knives in the world.

128
00:07:36,840 --> 00:07:40,880
It all starts with a steel alloy containing chromium.

129
00:07:40,881 --> 00:07:44,020
For the perfect balance of strength, edge retention,

130
00:07:44,120 --> 00:07:46,180
and corrosion resistance, the metal

131
00:07:46,280 --> 00:07:50,220
will undergo a high-tech heat treatment.

132
00:07:50,320 --> 00:07:53,160
We've got the finest steel, but if we don't go through

133
00:07:53,260 --> 00:07:55,900
a proper heat-treating process, we've wasted all our money.

134
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:58,200
This will just show you here, this is not heat-treated.

135
00:07:58,300 --> 00:08:01,140
So here's what we would have if we'd not heat-treat this blade.

136
00:08:01,240 --> 00:08:02,540
It would bend in your home.

137
00:08:02,640 --> 00:08:04,040
It would not hold an edge.

138
00:08:04,140 --> 00:08:06,540
In the first step of the brutal three-part heat-treat

139
00:08:06,640 --> 00:08:10,340
process, a conveyor feeds newly stamped undercut

140
00:08:10,341 --> 00:08:13,780
unsharpened blank blades to a furnace.

141
00:08:13,880 --> 00:08:15,920
Now we're going to put it in a deep freezer

142
00:08:16,020 --> 00:08:19,580
and freeze it to 120 degrees below zero for about two hours

143
00:08:19,680 --> 00:08:22,080
at temperature and pull it out.

144
00:08:22,180 --> 00:08:24,880
After these two steps, the cold steel blades

145
00:08:24,980 --> 00:08:27,360
are far from finished.

146
00:08:27,460 --> 00:08:30,700
What we would have now is a brittle part.

147
00:08:30,800 --> 00:08:32,200
And what you see here on the table

148
00:08:32,300 --> 00:08:36,360
is that here are some parts that have been heat treated and frozen,

149
00:08:36,460 --> 00:08:38,040
but they're very brittle.

150
00:08:38,041 --> 00:08:45,380
If I put this in, you can see here, it broke very easily.

151
00:08:45,480 --> 00:08:47,980
To overcome this problem, another furnace

152
00:08:48,080 --> 00:08:51,180
warms the steel again.

153
00:08:51,280 --> 00:08:54,620
That will give us a tough part, very much what we see right

154
00:08:54,720 --> 00:08:56,920
here in these knives that have been ground and finished.

155
00:08:57,020 --> 00:08:59,960
But you can see how they'll flex.

156
00:09:00,060 --> 00:09:02,200
Now that each blank has just the right steel

157
00:09:02,300 --> 00:09:06,360
consistency, it's time to grind the redges.

158
00:09:06,361 --> 00:09:10,160
Each blank, now 95,000ths of an inch thick,

159
00:09:10,260 --> 00:09:13,700
passes through an automatic grinder, which tapers its edge

160
00:09:13,800 --> 00:09:17,240
to just 18,000ths of an inch.

161
00:09:17,340 --> 00:09:19,840
This is about five times as thick as a sheet of paper.

162
00:09:22,780 --> 00:09:24,640
But we've only gotten started.

163
00:09:24,740 --> 00:09:26,340
Now here comes the process that'll

164
00:09:26,440 --> 00:09:28,780
turn these edges into the world's sharpest.

165
00:09:31,420 --> 00:09:35,320
Operators manually press each blade, one side at a time,

166
00:09:35,321 --> 00:09:38,820
along a powered, four-inch-wide moving conveyor belt

167
00:09:38,920 --> 00:09:43,000
with a consistency not unlike sandpaper.

168
00:09:43,100 --> 00:09:48,940
In seconds, a barely visible light line appears on the rim.

169
00:09:49,040 --> 00:09:51,700
This is called the burr.

170
00:09:51,800 --> 00:09:52,600
Need a better look?

171
00:09:55,140 --> 00:09:57,540
The honing process grinds down the metal

172
00:09:57,640 --> 00:10:00,980
until the two faces meet each other at the edge.

173
00:10:01,080 --> 00:10:03,320
Once they do, one side of the metal

174
00:10:03,321 --> 00:10:07,420
over the udder and produces the burr, which is a thin, nearly

175
00:10:07,520 --> 00:10:08,320
microscopic wire.

176
00:10:11,320 --> 00:10:14,060
It's just a floppy piece of thread like hanging on the edge.

177
00:10:14,160 --> 00:10:15,760
And actually, it bends back and forth.

178
00:10:15,860 --> 00:10:18,060
So you need to take it off until you get down

179
00:10:18,160 --> 00:10:20,580
to the strength of the edge.

180
00:10:20,680 --> 00:10:22,980
If the burr were not removed, the knife

181
00:10:23,080 --> 00:10:24,480
would act more like a saw.

182
00:10:27,820 --> 00:10:30,820
Polishing erases the burr.

183
00:10:30,821 --> 00:10:33,220
If you use a cloth buff, much as you'd

184
00:10:33,320 --> 00:10:37,800
use if you were polishing your car with an automatic buffer,

185
00:10:37,900 --> 00:10:40,600
the wheels are turning 3,600 RPM.

186
00:10:40,700 --> 00:10:43,100
So when they're turning, they're hard as a rock.

187
00:10:43,200 --> 00:10:45,900
What we're actually doing is polishing the edge, which

188
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:49,240
actually buffs away the burr and makes it very smooth,

189
00:10:49,340 --> 00:10:51,480
so the edge actually glides through the foods

190
00:10:51,580 --> 00:10:54,140
that it's cutting.

191
00:10:54,240 --> 00:10:59,440
Heated, ground, sharpened, and deburred.

192
00:10:59,441 --> 00:11:04,640
The cutting edge now measures a super thin 1 10,000th of an inch,

193
00:11:04,740 --> 00:11:06,680
20 times thinner than a human hair.

194
00:11:09,420 --> 00:11:12,180
But this minute edge isn't the only measure of a sharp

195
00:11:12,280 --> 00:11:15,660
and effective kitchen knife.

196
00:11:15,760 --> 00:11:20,520
It must also keep its edge for as long as possible.

197
00:11:20,620 --> 00:11:22,700
What good is a knife if it can only slice well

198
00:11:22,800 --> 00:11:23,860
through its first pineapple?

199
00:11:26,660 --> 00:11:29,440
To prove its knives are superior to other brands,

200
00:11:29,441 --> 00:11:34,180
Cutco conducts a Catra test, an industry standard.

201
00:11:34,280 --> 00:11:36,780
The test simulates wear on the blade

202
00:11:36,880 --> 00:11:39,720
by forcing it through thin cardboard strips

203
00:11:39,820 --> 00:11:41,820
filled with fine granules of sand.

204
00:11:45,360 --> 00:11:47,520
The test will measure not only the effectiveness

205
00:11:47,620 --> 00:11:51,900
of its first slice, but also of its 60th.

206
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:55,540
One of the important factors that the cutlery industry

207
00:11:55,541 --> 00:12:00,540
wants is how sharp is this knife going to be when you buy it?

208
00:12:00,640 --> 00:12:02,540
And, of course, the longevity.

209
00:12:02,640 --> 00:12:05,580
How long is that knife going to last?

210
00:12:05,680 --> 00:12:08,020
For some perspective, a butter knife

211
00:12:08,120 --> 00:12:10,380
takes the stage first.

212
00:12:10,480 --> 00:12:13,520
It doesn't exactly make the cut.

213
00:12:13,620 --> 00:12:17,300
Actually, it's supposed to be doing some cutting through

214
00:12:17,400 --> 00:12:18,900
on the test media.

215
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:22,300
About all it will do is probably cut butter.

216
00:12:22,301 --> 00:12:26,600
It's not going to get through maybe one of our test media

217
00:12:26,700 --> 00:12:29,500
after 60 times.

218
00:12:29,600 --> 00:12:32,240
Repeated tests help back up Cutco's claim

219
00:12:32,340 --> 00:12:34,540
that it's the sharpest knife you can find in a kitchen

220
00:12:34,640 --> 00:12:36,640
drawer.

221
00:12:36,740 --> 00:12:39,320
Out of package sharpness will give us an average

222
00:12:39,420 --> 00:12:42,560
of approximately 30 cuts.

223
00:12:42,660 --> 00:12:47,420
Total card cut after 60 times gives us approximately 400 cuts

224
00:12:47,520 --> 00:12:50,620
of this test media.

225
00:12:50,621 --> 00:12:52,620
Over time, however, even what's billed

226
00:12:52,720 --> 00:12:54,520
as the world's sharpest kitchen knife

227
00:12:54,620 --> 00:12:57,020
inevitably dulls them.

228
00:12:57,120 --> 00:13:00,960
But the food it's cutting isn't necessarily the reason.

229
00:13:01,060 --> 00:13:03,160
It's other things that the knife comes in contact

230
00:13:03,260 --> 00:13:04,460
that does the dulling.

231
00:13:04,560 --> 00:13:06,600
China plates or glass cutting boards

232
00:13:06,700 --> 00:13:10,040
or very hard other surfaces.

233
00:13:10,140 --> 00:13:11,980
To combat the dulling effect,

234
00:13:12,080 --> 00:13:15,440
Cutco manufactures a blade with an unusual edge

235
00:13:15,540 --> 00:13:19,140
they call the double D.

236
00:13:19,141 --> 00:13:22,180
Protruding points line the lower lip of the blade

237
00:13:22,280 --> 00:13:24,440
and protect the sharp cutting surfaces,

238
00:13:24,540 --> 00:13:27,980
which hide in upper U-shaped recesses.

239
00:13:28,080 --> 00:13:30,080
What's going on here is the points

240
00:13:30,180 --> 00:13:31,880
are what's touching the glass plate.

241
00:13:31,980 --> 00:13:35,020
So what's being dulled are the points,

242
00:13:35,120 --> 00:13:37,700
not the upper parts of the edge.

243
00:13:37,800 --> 00:13:39,860
Think these blades are sharp?

244
00:13:39,960 --> 00:13:43,260
Chances are you have a very close encounter every morning

245
00:13:43,360 --> 00:13:46,300
with a blade over 80 times sharper.

246
00:13:46,400 --> 00:13:48,740
Keep that hand steady.

247
00:13:49,140 --> 00:13:54,140
Two up, two up, two up, two up, two up, two up, two up.

248
00:13:54,240 --> 00:13:57,140
A century ago, the sharpest edge most men encountered

249
00:13:57,240 --> 00:13:59,140
was at their local barber shop.

250
00:13:59,240 --> 00:14:01,140
Two up, two up, whoa.

251
00:14:01,240 --> 00:14:04,140
There, skilled professionals gripped blades sharp enough

252
00:14:04,240 --> 00:14:09,140
to split hairs, hopefully without cutting flesh.

253
00:14:09,240 --> 00:14:13,140
Today, in some barber shops, little has changed.

254
00:14:13,240 --> 00:14:17,140
Keeping shaving blades razor sharp is an ongoing challenge,

255
00:14:17,141 --> 00:14:18,380
challenge.

256
00:14:18,381 --> 00:14:22,140
As shaving hair nicks steel and dulls them.

257
00:14:22,141 --> 00:14:25,320
But old pros like Damon Zilli have made honing them

258
00:14:25,321 --> 00:14:27,080
an art form.

259
00:14:27,081 --> 00:14:29,920
He manually grinds the straight razor over a double-sided

260
00:14:29,921 --> 00:14:34,220
honing stone to restore the damaged edge.

261
00:14:34,221 --> 00:14:36,360
The black side is more of a coarse side,

262
00:14:36,361 --> 00:14:40,300
and the brown side is more of a refined side.

263
00:14:40,301 --> 00:14:43,500
It's a little smoother, softer type of stone.

264
00:14:43,501 --> 00:14:45,160
So that's what I'm doing right now.

265
00:14:45,161 --> 00:14:48,600
I can't see it, but I'm just taking off the microscopic nicks

266
00:14:48,700 --> 00:14:51,760
on there.

267
00:14:51,860 --> 00:14:54,040
And just like the cutlery makers at Cutco

268
00:14:54,140 --> 00:14:56,600
polish the burr off of their knives,

269
00:14:56,700 --> 00:15:00,840
Damon needs to smooth his razor's edge.

270
00:15:00,940 --> 00:15:01,980
The first step right here is I'm

271
00:15:02,080 --> 00:15:03,620
going to strot with the canvas.

272
00:15:03,720 --> 00:15:06,920
So right now, it's just getting rid of the excess metal that

273
00:15:07,020 --> 00:15:09,580
was on the edge of my blade right here.

274
00:15:09,680 --> 00:15:11,080
So now I'm going to flip it over.

275
00:15:11,180 --> 00:15:14,760
And now I'm going to focus on the leather portion of the strot.

276
00:15:14,761 --> 00:15:17,660
Basically, it's a little finer finish,

277
00:15:17,760 --> 00:15:21,540
and it closes the pores and gaps and nits

278
00:15:21,640 --> 00:15:23,760
on the edge of my razor blade.

279
00:15:23,860 --> 00:15:25,880
When I didn't do the stropping, my customer

280
00:15:25,980 --> 00:15:27,240
wouldn't get a good shave, period.

281
00:15:31,280 --> 00:15:34,540
Over 200 years ago, the first steel straight razors

282
00:15:34,640 --> 00:15:38,220
were so expensive, most men had to visit a professional barber

283
00:15:38,320 --> 00:15:41,080
for a close shave.

284
00:15:41,180 --> 00:15:43,220
If I don't use the sharpest blade,

285
00:15:43,221 --> 00:15:46,920
He's going to walk out here an unhappy customer.

286
00:15:47,020 --> 00:15:50,020
But beginning in 1904, the safety razor

287
00:15:50,120 --> 00:15:54,500
armed us with disposable blades so sharp and so inexpensive

288
00:15:54,600 --> 00:15:58,660
that worldwide, we now spend $10 billion every year

289
00:15:58,760 --> 00:16:02,400
shaving ourselves at home.

290
00:16:02,500 --> 00:16:04,840
On these blades, we find the sharpest steel edges

291
00:16:04,940 --> 00:16:05,840
in the world.

292
00:16:08,540 --> 00:16:11,240
At this Schick plant in Connecticut,

293
00:16:11,241 --> 00:16:14,200
grinding millions of microscopically sharp edges,

294
00:16:14,300 --> 00:16:17,780
begins with a thin ribbon of steel.

295
00:16:17,880 --> 00:16:21,420
This steel is unique to the manufacturing of razor blades.

296
00:16:21,520 --> 00:16:23,560
And what makes it special for that application

297
00:16:23,660 --> 00:16:25,680
is the microstructure of this steel.

298
00:16:25,780 --> 00:16:27,920
It's made in such a way that we can

299
00:16:28,020 --> 00:16:31,420
grind a very fine edge on it.

300
00:16:31,520 --> 00:16:34,060
Each coil contains two miles of steel

301
00:16:34,160 --> 00:16:38,540
3,000ths of an inch thick, enough to produce 80,000 razor

302
00:16:38,640 --> 00:16:39,960
blades.

303
00:16:39,961 --> 00:16:44,600
Eraser blade is ground with three distinct facets or bevels.

304
00:16:44,700 --> 00:16:48,640
So it is not a straight edge that leads to the ultimate tip.

305
00:16:48,740 --> 00:16:50,800
A grinder crafts these three facets

306
00:16:50,900 --> 00:16:51,900
into the steel coil.

307
00:16:54,740 --> 00:16:56,880
Textured sandpaper-like grinding wheels

308
00:16:56,980 --> 00:16:59,680
mill the front and back of the coil simultaneously.

309
00:17:02,480 --> 00:17:05,860
In the first stage, very coarse wheels are used.

310
00:17:05,960 --> 00:17:08,920
These grinding wheels do most of the stock removal.

311
00:17:08,921 --> 00:17:12,380
That is to say, bring the blade to the width that you wanted.

312
00:17:12,880 --> 00:17:15,880
On the second stage, a finer grid wheel is used.

313
00:17:16,460 --> 00:17:20,780
And this second bevel, this is the preparation to the third and final stage.

314
00:17:21,100 --> 00:17:25,440
The final stage is ground using very fine grid wheels

315
00:17:25,441 --> 00:17:29,700
that leave a very smooth and continuous edge on the blade.

316
00:17:31,600 --> 00:17:33,920
Grinders also milled the opposite edge of the coil,

317
00:17:34,460 --> 00:17:36,600
resulting in a double-sided sharpened strip.

318
00:17:36,601 --> 00:17:41,600
Then a guillotine chops the steel into individual 1 1

319
00:17:41,700 --> 00:17:43,600
1 1 inch long blades.

320
00:17:43,700 --> 00:17:48,240
Here you can see the individual blade with sharp edges both

321
00:17:48,340 --> 00:17:49,840
from top and the bottom.

322
00:17:49,940 --> 00:17:53,360
Eventually, this blade will be cut in half,

323
00:17:53,460 --> 00:17:56,260
and this will be a single edge that will be assembled

324
00:17:56,360 --> 00:17:59,460
into a razor or cartridge.

325
00:17:59,560 --> 00:18:02,060
The edges on the world's sharpest razor blades

326
00:18:02,160 --> 00:18:05,960
measure just 30 nanometers thick.

327
00:18:05,961 --> 00:18:09,900
over 3,000 times thinner than a sheet of paper.

328
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:12,600
But surprisingly, they still aren't good enough to shave

329
00:18:12,700 --> 00:18:13,700
with.

330
00:18:13,800 --> 00:18:15,300
Why?

331
00:18:15,400 --> 00:18:17,900
Because they're not yet strong enough to take on a formidable

332
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:20,900
foe, human hair.

333
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:22,840
Our human hair is, in fact, very tough.

334
00:18:22,940 --> 00:18:25,640
The hardness is really equivalent to copper

335
00:18:25,740 --> 00:18:27,480
of the same diameter.

336
00:18:27,580 --> 00:18:30,820
You really want the edge of the blade to be as hard as possible,

337
00:18:30,920 --> 00:18:32,980
yet as sharp as possible.

338
00:18:33,080 --> 00:18:35,260
As sharp as these blades are,

339
00:18:35,261 --> 00:18:38,100
Schick has to fortify them with two separate coatings

340
00:18:38,200 --> 00:18:40,960
to make them practical.

341
00:18:41,060 --> 00:18:44,000
Inside this vacuum chamber, Schick's Quattro blades

342
00:18:44,100 --> 00:18:47,400
are coated with the company's hardest available covering,

343
00:18:47,500 --> 00:18:52,920
a combination of titanium and man-made diamond.

344
00:18:53,020 --> 00:18:57,320
And inside a second chamber, a low-friction polymer film

345
00:18:57,420 --> 00:19:00,920
is sprayed onto the cutting edge.

346
00:19:01,020 --> 00:19:03,020
The first coating process basically

347
00:19:03,021 --> 00:19:05,360
basically makes the edge harder.

348
00:19:05,361 --> 00:19:08,960
And the second process basically makes the edge slipperier,

349
00:19:08,961 --> 00:19:11,260
so that it glides through the hairs instead of pulling

350
00:19:11,261 --> 00:19:13,460
and tugging at them.

351
00:19:13,461 --> 00:19:16,600
Finally, the blades move to the assembly department.

352
00:19:16,601 --> 00:19:19,060
There, a high-speed automated process

353
00:19:19,061 --> 00:19:22,080
mounts the blades to plastic components

354
00:19:22,081 --> 00:19:25,880
and packages the completed disposable razors.

355
00:19:25,881 --> 00:19:28,280
They're now ready to bring a close shave to the masses.

356
00:19:28,281 --> 00:19:34,520
Of course, their edges aren't called razor sharp for nothing.

357
00:19:34,521 --> 00:19:38,520
Under the unforgiving eye of the microscope,

358
00:19:38,521 --> 00:19:41,960
the edge of a paring knife sample looks like a jagged mess

359
00:19:41,961 --> 00:19:43,460
compared to a razor blade.

360
00:19:43,461 --> 00:19:48,460
We're only at 150 times magnification.

361
00:19:48,461 --> 00:19:51,640
You can clearly see the edge of the knife.

362
00:19:51,641 --> 00:19:56,040
But over here on the left, the razor blade is almost invisible

363
00:19:56,041 --> 00:19:57,040
at this point.

364
00:19:57,041 --> 00:19:58,200
at this point.

365
00:19:58,201 --> 00:20:01,580
And that's simply because the razor blade tip is so much finer

366
00:20:01,581 --> 00:20:02,780
than the knife.

367
00:20:02,781 --> 00:20:07,040
This is the sharpest edge you can get on steel.

368
00:20:07,041 --> 00:20:10,960
That may be true, but no steel blade can do the job

369
00:20:10,961 --> 00:20:17,320
of these ultra sharp cutters, slicing hour after hour

370
00:20:17,321 --> 00:20:20,160
through this tough plastic film that will eventually

371
00:20:20,161 --> 00:20:22,800
be transformed into your household plastic wrap.

372
00:20:22,801 --> 00:20:27,280
Produced by specialty blades in Virginia,

373
00:20:27,380 --> 00:20:30,740
they may look like ordinary steel,

374
00:20:30,840 --> 00:20:33,540
but they're made of zirconia ceramic.

375
00:20:33,640 --> 00:20:36,620
Their edges are far more slippery than steel,

376
00:20:36,720 --> 00:20:41,380
enabling them to stay razor sharp up to 100 times longer.

377
00:20:41,480 --> 00:20:45,120
The film itself is rubbing against the sides of the

378
00:20:45,220 --> 00:20:46,820
ceramic right past the edge.

379
00:20:46,920 --> 00:20:50,120
And if you have a material with a low coefficient of friction,

380
00:20:50,121 --> 00:20:53,360
friction, the film does not get stretched,

381
00:20:53,460 --> 00:20:55,820
and you get a better cut.

382
00:20:55,920 --> 00:20:59,860
The other thing that makes ceramic good in film slitting

383
00:20:59,960 --> 00:21:04,660
is it's a really hard material, so we can put a fine edge on it

384
00:21:04,760 --> 00:21:07,180
without getting burrs.

385
00:21:07,280 --> 00:21:11,180
Zirconia ceramic is about six times harder than steel.

386
00:21:11,280 --> 00:21:13,380
But it's also more brittle.

387
00:21:13,480 --> 00:21:17,020
We never recommend a ceramic blade in a high impact

388
00:21:17,120 --> 00:21:20,120
application, because the ceramic edge will chip.

389
00:21:20,121 --> 00:21:23,720
Just like the process that sharpens steel razor blades,

390
00:21:23,820 --> 00:21:26,320
pairs of grinding wheels inside this housing

391
00:21:26,420 --> 00:21:31,260
hone ceramic simultaneously on two sides,

392
00:21:31,360 --> 00:21:33,960
but with one crucial difference.

393
00:21:34,060 --> 00:21:36,160
The sharpening of ceramic requires

394
00:21:36,260 --> 00:21:39,180
the use of diamond grinding wheels and hones.

395
00:21:39,280 --> 00:21:43,180
When you get into something as hard as zirconia ceramic,

396
00:21:43,280 --> 00:21:44,880
you have to have a grinding wheel that's

397
00:21:44,980 --> 00:21:47,780
harder than the material being sharpened.

398
00:21:47,781 --> 00:21:49,920
One of the things that surprises a lot of people

399
00:21:50,020 --> 00:21:53,060
about these blades is how sharp they are right when they come

400
00:21:53,160 --> 00:21:54,260
out of the machine.

401
00:21:54,360 --> 00:21:56,260
For example, here's a piece of notebook paper,

402
00:21:56,360 --> 00:21:59,760
and this blade will just forever come in

403
00:21:59,860 --> 00:22:02,900
and cut little pieces off of it.

404
00:22:03,000 --> 00:22:04,900
Try that with your pocket knife sometime

405
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:08,160
and see if it'll work.

406
00:22:08,260 --> 00:22:09,900
Yes, getting through a piece of paper

407
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:12,500
is not so tough with the right blade.

408
00:22:12,600 --> 00:22:14,280
But which of the world's sharpest

409
00:22:14,281 --> 00:22:19,180
would you need to get through something like a fridge?

410
00:22:19,280 --> 00:22:23,120
How about two miles worth of overpowering jumbo scissors?

411
00:22:30,300 --> 00:22:35,860
Meet the BC-1800XL manufactured by Vermeer,

412
00:22:35,960 --> 00:22:41,640
an 8,700-pound wood-chipping brute vying for the title

413
00:22:41,641 --> 00:22:45,140
of the world's sharpest yard tool.

414
00:22:45,240 --> 00:22:48,340
It can clue your backyard of a forest full of fallen trees

415
00:22:48,440 --> 00:22:54,080
or overgrown bushes and pulverize it into mulch.

416
00:22:54,180 --> 00:22:59,660
This monster devours each mouthful of wood effortlessly,

417
00:22:59,760 --> 00:23:03,000
thanks in part to its belly full of ultra sharp steel alloy

418
00:23:03,100 --> 00:23:04,900
knives.

419
00:23:05,000 --> 00:23:07,400
This drum here has four knives on it.

420
00:23:07,500 --> 00:23:10,940
As I slowly rotate this, you'll see that they're staggered.

421
00:23:10,941 --> 00:23:14,240
offset to each other at approximately 90 degrees

422
00:23:14,340 --> 00:23:15,580
in relation to the drums.

423
00:23:15,680 --> 00:23:17,940
In relation to what we're used to in everyday life,

424
00:23:18,040 --> 00:23:20,380
it's probably sharper than any knife

425
00:23:20,480 --> 00:23:22,920
that you would find in the average household of America

426
00:23:23,020 --> 00:23:23,980
today.

427
00:23:24,080 --> 00:23:25,660
These super sharp edges will make

428
00:23:25,760 --> 00:23:30,520
well over 2,000 separate cuts into a 60-foot-long tree trunk

429
00:23:30,620 --> 00:23:33,060
as it passes through the chipper.

430
00:23:33,160 --> 00:23:37,440
They get a big assist from a 140-horsepower diesel engine.

431
00:23:37,540 --> 00:23:39,460
Velocity is one of the key factors

432
00:23:39,461 --> 00:23:41,000
in the chipping process.

433
00:23:41,100 --> 00:23:42,560
Without the velocity, we're not going

434
00:23:42,660 --> 00:23:46,500
to have the power to impact the wood.

435
00:23:46,600 --> 00:23:48,500
The engine feeds enough power and torque

436
00:23:48,600 --> 00:23:52,700
to the drum to rotate it 1,100 times every minute,

437
00:23:52,800 --> 00:23:54,900
or over 18 times every second.

438
00:23:58,920 --> 00:24:02,120
Two vertical feed rollers grip the incoming material

439
00:24:02,220 --> 00:24:05,220
and guide it toward the 36-inch diameter rotating drum.

440
00:24:05,221 --> 00:24:08,120
drum.

441
00:24:08,220 --> 00:24:11,920
As each knife mounted on the drum surface impacts the wood,

442
00:24:12,020 --> 00:24:15,000
it snips off a chip when it reaches a base-mounted shear

443
00:24:15,100 --> 00:24:16,700
bar.

444
00:24:16,800 --> 00:24:19,700
The distance between the shear bar and the cutting knives

445
00:24:19,800 --> 00:24:22,000
determines the chip size.

446
00:24:22,100 --> 00:24:23,100
There's paddles.

447
00:24:23,200 --> 00:24:25,040
What that does is creates airflow.

448
00:24:25,140 --> 00:24:26,600
We need a tremendous amount of airflow

449
00:24:26,700 --> 00:24:29,820
to create the discharge and keep the chips moving out.

450
00:24:32,520 --> 00:24:35,220
After about 40 hours of this punishing labor,

451
00:24:35,221 --> 00:24:37,960
the knife's edges begin to dull.

452
00:24:38,060 --> 00:24:40,660
The machine isn't designed to hammer or pound through the logs.

453
00:24:40,760 --> 00:24:42,060
It's designed to cut through, and that's

454
00:24:42,160 --> 00:24:46,160
where you need a very sharp, consistent tool to do that.

455
00:24:46,260 --> 00:24:50,760
Restoring the cutting edge requires some expert treatment.

456
00:24:50,860 --> 00:24:55,380
This machine sharpens blades, which I have right here.

457
00:24:55,480 --> 00:24:59,280
And this will be in here, like so.

458
00:24:59,380 --> 00:25:03,380
There's a stone that rotates clockwise,

459
00:25:03,381 --> 00:25:09,120
And it moves along, and it just sharpens just like that.

460
00:25:09,220 --> 00:25:11,360
The sharpener grinds away at the blade

461
00:25:11,460 --> 00:25:13,460
and refreshes its 30-degree angle

462
00:25:13,560 --> 00:25:15,160
and restores its pristine edge.

463
00:25:18,300 --> 00:25:20,760
Brush chippers like the BC-1800XL

464
00:25:20,860 --> 00:25:22,900
can plow through this mass of vegetation

465
00:25:23,000 --> 00:25:24,300
as fast as you can feed it.

466
00:25:26,800 --> 00:25:28,540
But another set of powered blade cutters

467
00:25:28,640 --> 00:25:31,500
redefines the notion of ultra-sharpness

468
00:25:31,501 --> 00:25:37,740
by cutting not just through wood, but anything else.

469
00:25:37,840 --> 00:25:41,140
And we mean anything.

470
00:25:41,240 --> 00:25:45,320
Enter the almighty Primax, manufactured by SSI

471
00:25:45,420 --> 00:25:46,640
Shredding Systems of Oregon.

472
00:25:49,960 --> 00:25:52,480
The Primax was called on to clear the debris from New

473
00:25:52,580 --> 00:25:55,720
Orleans after Hurricane Katrina.

474
00:25:55,820 --> 00:25:57,820
It's the perfect tool for demolition sites

475
00:25:57,920 --> 00:25:59,420
and huge recycling projects.

476
00:26:01,500 --> 00:26:06,500
It can reduce unwanted bulk to a fraction of its original mass.

477
00:26:08,500 --> 00:26:10,500
Well, here's what's left over from the sofa.

478
00:26:10,501 --> 00:26:15,500
You have a chunk of the fabric, a piece of the back cushion.

479
00:26:15,501 --> 00:26:18,500
Here's the foam that you actually would sit on,

480
00:26:18,501 --> 00:26:21,500
and a big chunk of the wood from the bottom of it.

481
00:26:21,501 --> 00:26:24,500
This machine will just process it all.

482
00:26:24,501 --> 00:26:26,500
Inside the Primax's gaping mouth,

483
00:26:26,501 --> 00:26:30,500
two parallel shafts each hold six 3-inch thickets

484
00:26:30,501 --> 00:26:33,200
three-inch-thick steel circular cutters,

485
00:26:33,300 --> 00:26:34,500
rimmed with pointed hooks.

486
00:26:39,940 --> 00:26:43,240
These things are really designed to shred almost anything.

487
00:26:43,340 --> 00:26:45,480
These cutters have a piercing point on them.

488
00:26:45,580 --> 00:26:47,280
They literally pierce the material,

489
00:26:47,380 --> 00:26:49,080
and then the material is brought down

490
00:26:49,180 --> 00:26:50,920
into the root of the cutter, where

491
00:26:51,020 --> 00:26:53,020
the actual torque is transferred into it.

492
00:26:53,120 --> 00:26:57,520
And then it uses just brute force to break the material.

493
00:26:57,620 --> 00:26:59,020
That brute force is supplied

494
00:26:59,021 --> 00:27:03,260
by a 700-horsepower diesel engine.

495
00:27:03,360 --> 00:27:06,460
It enables the shredder to digest up to 150 tons

496
00:27:06,560 --> 00:27:09,660
of material every hour.

497
00:27:09,760 --> 00:27:11,260
The machine's really just designed

498
00:27:11,360 --> 00:27:12,760
to take the airspace out of the material,

499
00:27:12,860 --> 00:27:15,680
break it down, and prepare it for either transportation

500
00:27:15,780 --> 00:27:17,680
or recycling operations.

501
00:27:17,780 --> 00:27:19,480
But some recycling operations,

502
00:27:19,580 --> 00:27:23,180
like those processing rubber tires and plastic products,

503
00:27:23,280 --> 00:27:25,520
require shredders that can break down material

504
00:27:25,620 --> 00:27:29,020
into much smaller pieces.

505
00:27:29,021 --> 00:27:33,620
This makes it easier to process the material into something new.

506
00:27:33,720 --> 00:27:36,460
The answer is a shredder like this one,

507
00:27:36,560 --> 00:27:38,560
with blades positioned much closer together

508
00:27:38,660 --> 00:27:40,560
than those of the Primax.

509
00:27:40,660 --> 00:27:42,860
You have two cutters here, and in between them

510
00:27:42,960 --> 00:27:47,380
is a surface that's just exactly like between the two surfaces

511
00:27:47,480 --> 00:27:49,380
of the scissors here.

512
00:27:49,480 --> 00:27:52,080
What we have is a whole bunch of what I call shear pairs,

513
00:27:52,180 --> 00:27:54,080
which are just exactly like scissors.

514
00:27:54,180 --> 00:27:57,680
And the shafts are turning, and they force those scissors

515
00:27:57,681 --> 00:28:00,320
to come together right there at the joint,

516
00:28:00,840 --> 00:28:02,680
and material falling in there,

517
00:28:02,880 --> 00:28:04,660
it's just like having a whole line of scissors

518
00:28:04,661 --> 00:28:06,300
and a whole bunch of people just snip, snip, snip, snip,

519
00:28:06,301 --> 00:28:09,500
all simultaneously doing that as it goes across.

520
00:28:09,680 --> 00:28:11,380
One of the things that can happen is

521
00:28:11,381 --> 00:28:13,780
when you're cutting paper,

